  "title": "Prime-Gap Dyadic Series: Perturbations, Exact Criteria and Certificates",
  "subject": "Erdős #251",
  "subject_kind": "problem",
  "form": "Reasoning surface",
  "pages": 46,
  "question": "What exact tail criteria, sparse countermodels, and finite denominator bounds are proved for the prime-gap dyadic series, and why is irrationality still open?",
  "precis": "A convergent dyadic series with nonnegative integer coefficients can be made rational by sparse nonnegative corrections of upper Banach density zero, eventually bounded by any f(n)→∞, while preserving every fixed modulus and, for a logarithmic allowance, short-block empirical distributions. For prime gaps the cumulative positions remain asymptotic to n log n; no primality conclusion is asserted. Summation by parts reduces the prime series to the gap series, and the tails T_N=∑_{j≥1} g_{N+j} 2^{-j} satisfy T_{N+1}=2T_N−g_{N+1}. That recurrence characterises rationality and yields finite tests with explicit remainder bounds. An exact finite certificate shows that a rational value of the prime series would have denominator at least 2^{39998}>10^{12040}. The bound does not establish irrationality.",
  "owns": "the complete problem-specific reasoning surface for Erdős #251, including all registered result families and their boundaries",
  "not_authority_for": "the validity of claims tagged Lean, which belongs to the cited kernel-checked source, or a solution to Erdős #251, which remains open",
